Let $Q$ denote a nonempty, closed, and convex subset of a real Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$. Consider $S:Q\rightarrow Q$ as a nonexpansive mapping, $A:Q\rightarrow Q$ as an inverse strongly monotone operator, and $f:Q\rightarrow Q$ as a contraction mapping. We demonstrate that, under suitable conditions on the real sequences $\{\alpha_{n}\}$ and $\{\lambda_{n}\}$, the sequence $\{x_{n}\}$ initiated at any $x_{1}\in Q$ via the iterative scheme
\begin{equation}
x_{n+1}=\alpha_{n}f(x_{n})+(1-\alpha_{n})SP_{Q}(x_{n}-\lambda_{n}Ax_{n})
\label{Alg} \end{equation}
converges strongly to a specific element within the set $F_{ix}(S)\cap S_{VI(A,Q)}$, which we assume to be nonempty. Here, $F_{ix}(S)$ represents the set of fixed points of $S$, and $S_{VI(A,Q)}$ denotes the set of points $q\in Q$ satisfying $\langle Aq,x-q\rangle\geq0$ for all $x\in Q$. Additionally, we investigate the strong convergence of a perturbed variant of this algorithm. Furthermore, we utilize the primary result to develop an algorithm for a constrained convex optimization problem and present a numerical experiment highlighting the impact of the parameter sequence $\{\alpha_{n}\}$ on the algorithm's convergence speed.

