Given a constant $\alpha > 0$, an integer $\ell \ge 0$, and the classical Euler gamma function $\Gamma(z)$, the authors utilize the integral representation of the Riemann zeta function $\zeta(z)$, a monotonicity principle for integral ratios with parameters, and properties concerning complete monotonicity and the function $\frac{1}{e^t-1}$ (along with its derivatives) to establish the following results:
(1) For any $\ell \ge 0$, the function $x \mapsto \binom{x+\alpha+\ell}{\alpha}\frac{\zeta(x+\alpha)}{\zeta(x)}$ is strictly increasing on the interval $(1,\infty)$, mapping onto $(0,\infty)$, where $\binom{z}{w}$ represents the extended binomial coefficient;
(2) For any $\ell \ge 1$, the function $x \mapsto \Gamma(x+\ell)\zeta(x)$ exhibits logarithmic convexity on $(1,\infty)$.
